00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:06.110 align:start position:0%
less than a billion years ago life  crossed a threshold single cells started  to band together eventually evolving  into the riot of shapes and functions of  multicellular life today it's been  thought that the evolution of  multicellularity must have been a major  genetic leap but new research suggests  that it wasn't such a difficult  transition after all scientists looked  at an organism that seems closest to  making this leap  Carano flageolets while single-celled  resemble the specialized cells that line  the channels of sponges which are  considered by many to be the most  primitive living animal a lab found 350  gene families in Kona flageolets once  thought to be exclusive to multicellular  animals the findings suggest that this  single-celled organism is already  equipped for multicellular life  a single cells life is simple but  limited like Hermits microbes need only  be concerned with feeding themselves in  contrast cells in a multicellular  organism give up their independence they  take on specialized functions they  sacrifice their progeny for the greater  good  take vulvar and algae which comes in a  spectrum of cellularity a lab compared  the biology and genetics of this diverse  set of algae and learned that as cells  banded together existing genes took on  new functions clam atom Onis is a  single-celled algae that uses its  centrioles for both swimming and  reproduction so it can't do both at the  same time it's multicellular relative  Volvox however can do both at once  because its cells have specialized with  some using the centriole for swimming  and others using it for reproduction  yeast is a fungus consisting of a single  cell one lab conducted a series of test  tube experiments with yeast cultures to  better understand how cells evolved  increasing complexity the biggest cells  which were the ones that settle to the  bottom of the flask were allowed to  survive and reproduce within two months  daughter cells stuck to their mothers  and form multicellular structures  dubbed snowflakes because of their shape  over time they got bigger and more and  more complex in this sped up simulation  of evolution the yeast specialized cells  in large snowflakes certain cells  undergo a type of suicide releasing the  cells at the tip to start a new  snowflake the dying cell sacrifices its  life so that the group can reproduce a  hallmark of multicellularity the team  conducted the same experiment with  chlamydomonas and again a kind of  multicellularity was quick to appear  scientists are still debating what drove  simple cells to become more and more  complex but with these genetic  comparisons and clever experiments what  was once thought as a major leap can now  be seen as a series of small genetic  steps that led to the wondrous diversity  of multicellular life today  [Music]  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

